/*
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=300
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=()
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' https://cdn.jsdelivr.net https://static.cloudflareinsights.com; style-src 'self' https://fonts.googleapis.com; font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com; img-src 'self' data: blob: https://xpxjqyraizntbtijzoyp.supabase.co; connect-src 'self' https://xpxjqyraizntbtijzoyp.supabase.co https://cloudflareinsights.com; object-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'

# Strict-Transport-Security is being rolled out in stages rather than
# shipped at a long duration immediately: max-age=300 (5 minutes) is
# Stage 1 — long enough to prove the header is served correctly and that
# no HTTP-only asset/redirect breaks under it, short enough that if
# something DOES break, the mistake self-heals within minutes instead of
# being locked in for weeks/months (a long max-age + includeSubDomains
# is very hard to safely undo once real browsers have cached it). Stage
# 2/3 (a longer duration, then includeSubDomains, then eventually
# preload submission) are deliberately deferred to a later milestone
# once Stage 1 has run in production without incident — see
# docs/DEPLOYMENT.md for the staged rollout plan. Do not add
# includeSubDomains or preload, and do not raise max-age, in this PR.

# img-src's blob: (added alongside the pre-existing 'self'/data:/Supabase
# origin, nothing else changed) — js/services/imageService.js's
# loadImage() decodes a locally-selected file by setting an <img>'s src
# to URL.createObjectURL(file), a blob: URL, purely to read the file's
# real pixel dimensions/decode it into a canvas before ever uploading
# anything. Without blob: here, the browser blocks that local preview
# outright (this app never called it "unsafe" — the browser did, at the
# CSP layer, before the image ever reached Supabase Storage), which
# surfaced to users as "That file could not be read as an image." for
# every avatar and gallery upload, valid files included. blob: is a
# browser-generated, same-session, this-tab-only URL scheme (not a
# remote origin an attacker can serve content from), so this does not
# admit any new external image source — deliberately NOT added to
# script-src or connect-src, which is what would actually broaden what
# code can run or where data can be sent.

# script-src's https://static.cloudflareinsights.com and connect-src's
# https://cloudflareinsights.com (deliberately different subdomains —
# not a typo) allow exactly Cloudflare Pages' own "Web Analytics"
# beacon, which Cloudflare injects into every page's HTML response at
# the edge (it is NOT present in this repo's own page source — grep
# won't find it here). It loads
# https://static.cloudflareinsights.com/beacon.min.js (plus a
# same-origin versioned sub-resource under that same path, e.g.
# .../beacon.min.js/v<hash>) and, once loaded, reports each pageview to
# https://cloudflareinsights.com/cdn-cgi/rum — a bare-domain endpoint
# per Cloudflare's own documented Web Analytics CSP guidance, hence the
# separate connect-src entry rather than reusing the static. origin.
# Confirmed via production console CSP violations during the anonymous
# smoke test that first found this whole defect: the beacon script tag
# itself is external (`<script defer src="https://static.
# cloudflareinsights.com/beacon.min.js" data-cf-beacon="...">`), so
# these two narrow origin allowances are sufficient — no 'unsafe-inline'
# needed for this. The SEPARATE inline-script violations seen in that
# same console output were this app's own category/legal page bootstrap
# blocks, fixed directly (see js/pages/categories/bootstrap.js and
# js/pages/legal/bootstrap.js), not a CSP change.

# Cloudflare Pages applies its own default Cache-Control (public,
# max-age=14400, must-revalidate — a 4-hour browser cache) to static
# assets that this file doesn't otherwise cover. That default is what
# let an already-open Safari tab keep rendering a pre-deploy CSS/JS
# bundle for up to four hours after a new deploy went live (confirmed
# on a real device: a fresh Private tab got the current navbar, an
# existing regular tab didn't). Cloudflare's own per-deploy edge-cache
# invalidation isn't the issue here — this is the BROWSER honoring the
# max-age it was told, independent of that. Cloudflare Pages combines
# ALL matching rules for a request rather than picking one "most
# specific" match; when more than one matching rule sets the SAME
# header, their values are joined with a comma. That only matters here
# if /* also set Cache-Control — it doesn't, so /css/* and /js/* below
# simply add Cache-Control (nothing to comma-join), while still
# picking up every header /* DOES set (the security headers above) via
# the same combining behavior, since those header names aren't
# repeated here.
/css/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/js/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
